    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear focal point in **Corbin Carroll** (80.29), who headlines our outfield. With a favorable matchup against Michael Lorenzen and a robust 0.374 xwOBA, **Corbin Carroll** is our highest-projected asset; his elite sprint speed remains our primary competitive advantage in a league where stolen bases are the scarcest commodity. He is joined at the top of the board by **Brice Turang** (74.07), who benefits significantly from the matchup against Roki Sasaki. Despite the SP-heavy volatility of the slate, **Brice Turang** provides essential categorical depth, aided by a high-leverage spot in the batting order. We are also leaning heavily on the upside of **James Wood** (68.19) and **Josh Naylor** (69.12) to anchor the counting categories; **James Wood** is particularly well-positioned today with a premium spot in the #1 hole against Grant Holmes, while **Josh Naylor** thrives in the favorable dynamic platoon split against Stephen Kolek.\n\nThe most difficult decision on today\u2019s bench was excluding **Kyle Karros** (51.32), who narrowly missed the cut behind lower-tier starts like **Cole Young**. While **Kyle Karros** offers a solid floor, the optimizer prioritized the volume and situational efficiency of our current utility rotation. We are operating with caution regarding the uncertainty surrounding **Gabriel Moreno** and **Dalton Rushing**, both of whom are \"Lineup Pending\" and currently suppressing their scores due to the volatility of late-order placement. We are also wary of **Vinnie Pasquantino** (57.72); while he maintains a strong platoon edge against George Kirby, Kirby\u2019s elite arm (3.47 xERA) introduces significant downside risk to our batting average goals. We continue to monitor the IL status of **Mickey Moniak** and the postponement affecting **Jose Caballero**, adjusting our daily strategy to ensure we maintain roster integrity as we navigate a high-variance schedule.",
